    {
      "title": "Show HN: Open-source ESP32 desk body for a local AI assistant",
      "summary": "Here is a fun project if you want to make an open-source Meta Muse Charm. It is a cheap ESP32-S3 talking toy from Shanghai talking to my Mac/Linux desktop app (BoBe), with zero API keys on the chip. I mapped the screen, audio and power-latch pins with no schematic, and wired a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 to press its physical buttons automatically behind a packet-loss router to test bad Wi-Fi. Not selling anything, just a hobby project, all is free and open source on GitHub, if you find it interesting feel free to use it: https://johnkozaris.com/blog/bobe-toy-robot-body",
      "summary_html": "<p>Here is a fun project if you want to make an open-source Meta Muse Charm.<p>It is a cheap ESP32-S3 talking toy from Shanghai talking to my Mac/Linux desktop app (BoBe), with zero API keys on the chip. I mapped the screen, audio and power-latch pins with no schematic, and wired a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 to press its physical buttons automatically behind a packet-loss router to test bad Wi-Fi.<p>Not selling anything, just a hobby project, all is free and open source on GitHub, if you find it interesting feel free to use it: <a href=\"https://johnkozaris.com/blog/bobe-toy-robot-body\">https://johnkozaris.com/blog/bobe-toy-robot-body</a></p>\n\n<p>Comments URL: <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50668024\">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50668024</a></p>\n<p>Points: 2</p>\n<p># Comments: 0</p></p></p>",
